# Companion Scan configuration (documentation only — none of these are required).
# Vite reads process.env; the Deno server reads Deno.env. Export them in your
# shell or a systemd EnvironmentFile (see docs/DEPLOY.md); .env files are NOT
# loaded automatically.

# HTTPS hostname phones use to reach this machine. Auto-detected from
# `tailscale status --json` (or a <host>.ts.net.crt in the repo root) when unset.
#TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME=your-machine.your-tailnet.ts.net
# Same value, checked second (after TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME) by both main.ts's
# resolveHostname() and vite.config.ts's — set this instead if your deploy
# tooling names the variable VITE_HOSTNAME rather than TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME.
#VITE_HOSTNAME=your-machine.your-tailnet.ts.net

# Origin prefix accepted by the Deno server's CORS check and used for share
# links. Defaults to https://$TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME, else the historical dev host.
#ALLOWED_ORIGIN_PREFIX=https://your-machine.your-tailnet.ts.net

# Deno API server port (default 8000) and the target the Vite proxy forwards
# to (default http://localhost:8000) — override both to run isolated instances.
#PORT=8000
#API_TARGET=http://localhost:8000

# Vite dev/preview server port (default 5173). Read by main.ts (share/QR links),
# vite.config.ts (server.port/preview.port), scripts/qr.mjs, scripts/house-links.mjs,
# scripts/logs-watch.mjs, and playwright.config.ts's default base URL — same
# variable, same default, everywhere. Override to run an isolated instance.
#VITE_PORT=5173

# Where the Deno server reads/writes persisted state. Defaults are relative to
# the server's cwd; override to point at a different data volume.
#SCANS_DIR=./scans
#SUMMARIES_DIR=./summaries
#SESSIONS_DIR=./scans/sessions
#WORLDS_DIR=./worlds
# projects.json path (Project metadata for the World Editor's project picker).
#PROJECTS_FILE=./projects.json
# Reference photos and other Project-scoped media (POST /projects/:id/reference-photos).
# Defaults under SCANS_DIR so a single data-volume override still catches it.
#PROJECT_MEDIA_DIR=./scans/projects

# Security: world routes (owner/editor/viewer/scanner share-link auth) allow
# anonymous access by default — set to 1 to require a valid per-world member
# token on every route that doesn't explicitly allow anonymous. Off by default
# for solo/dev use; turn on for any shared or multi-tenant deployment.
#WORLD_STRICT_AUTH=1

# A scan whose geo distance from the world's origin exceeds this radius
# (meters) is not rejected — it's placed at a fallback grid position and
# flagged geoOutlier so the viewer can call it out instead of silently
# scattering scans across a huge, wrongly-placed world.
#WORLD_SCAN_MAX_RADIUS_M=250

# Log every CORS origin check to the console (origin seen vs. ALLOWED_ORIGIN_PREFIX).
# Noisy — leave off unless debugging a rejected phone/browser origin.
#DEBUG_CORS=1

# Inference engine: ollama (default) | onnx | mock
#INFERENCE_MODE=ollama
#OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
#OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3-vl:4b

# Context window for the ollama call (default 8192). Left unset, ollama uses the
# model's full window — 262144 for qwen3-vl:4b — which reserves ~44 GB of KV
# cache and spills onto CPU, turning a ~20 s capture into 100+ s.
#OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=8192

# Experimental WebRTC live view. Disabled by default because the current
# device-to-editor connection is not reliable. This flag gates both the scanner
# and editor UI and makes the Deno signaling route return 404 while it is off.
#VITE_ENABLE_WEBRTC_LIVE=false

# Experimental Place Composer, which manages buildings, floors, rooms, and
# place relationships from the Spatial Editor. Disabled until that workflow is
# ready for general use.
#VITE_ENABLE_PLACE_COMPOSER=false

# /infer request cap in bytes (default 12000000)
#INFER_MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=12000000

# ONNX inference-mode model/tokenizer paths (unused in the default "ollama"
# mode). Normally set only by the Dockerfile/systemd unit alongside a mounted
# model volume — leave unset for dev.
#MODEL_DIR=./public/models/LiquidAI/LFM2.5-VL-1.6B-ONNX/onnx/
#TOKENIZER_DIR=./public/models/LiquidAI/LFM2.5-VL-1.6B-ONNX/

# Pre-built static viewer bundle main.ts falls through to in production (Docker
# / Hub install). Normally set only by the Dockerfile/systemd unit — leave
# unset for dev, where Vite serves the UI directly.
#STATIC_DIR=./dist

# Playwright: base URL for browser tests (defaults to https://$TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME:5173)
#VITE_URL=https://your-machine.your-tailnet.ts.net:5173
# Chromium binary override for hosts where `npx playwright install` is unsupported
#CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH=~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-1217/chrome-linux64/chrome
# Test-only Deno test gate (splat_train.test.ts) — set to 1 to run the real
# end-to-end Brush training test instead of skipping it. Not read by the
# server; irrelevant unless you're running `deno task test` with a built
# brush-cli on a GPU box.
#BRUSH_E2E=1

# ── 4D person capture ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The 4d-capture subsystem (subsystems/4d-capture/) — the ONLY config here that
# points at a machine other than this one. It needs a CUDA GPU with ~44 GiB on a
# single device; no host on the current fleet qualifies, so unset is the expected
# state today. Unset is fully supported: person-4d-reconstruct jobs complete as
# 'skipped' with a remediation hint, exactly like BRUSH_BIN and LINGBOT_MAP_*.
#FDANYONE_HOST=https://gpu-box.your-tailnet.ts.net:9040
#FDANYONE_TOKEN=
# Synthesized views per orbit layer. MUST be divisible by 4 or 6 — validated
# before the job is submitted, because a bad value fails deep inside the model.
#FDANYONE_VIEWS_PER_LAYER=6
# Timesteps sliced per second of clip. Sequence length = duration x this, and
# each timestep costs a full splat train, so this is the cost budget.
# ADOPTED, NOT MEASURED — revisit after the first real run.
#FDANYONE_TIMESTEP_FPS=5
#FDANYONE_POLL_MS=15000
#FDANYONE_MAX_WAIT_MS=2700000
# Base URL the subsystem fetches clips from. Defaults to this server's Tailscale
# name on $PORT; set it when the subsystem reaches this host by another name.
#FDANYONE_CALLBACK_BASE=https://this-box.your-tailnet.ts.net:8000

# Upload caps. INGEST_MAX_VIDEO_BYTES also bounds a person clip, which is kept
# on disk rather than discarded after keyframe extraction — see
# docs/pipelines/4d-person.md for the growth characteristic.
#INGEST_MAX_VIDEO_BYTES=1500000000
#INGEST_MAX_PHOTOS_BYTES=500000000
# Blur gate for both ingest scripts (ingest-video.mjs / ingest-photos.mjs):
# frames below this variance-of-Laplacian are dropped, 0 disables, default 60.
# The per-run `--min-sharpness` flag wins over it. Both scripts refuse to drop
# EVERY frame — if the whole clip is below the gate the frames are kept and a
# warning is logged, so a bad threshold cannot silently produce an empty session.
#INGEST_MIN_SHARPNESS=60

# LingBot-Map reconstruction worker (optional; jobs report a hint when unset)
#LINGBOT_MAP_PYTHON=~/.venvs/lingbot-map/bin/python
#LINGBOT_MAP_MODEL_PATH=~/models/lingbot-map/checkpoint.pt
# Override the worker script location main.ts spawns (default:
# scripts/lingbot_map_worker.py next to main.ts) — useful for read-only deploys.
#LINGBOT_MAP_SCRIPT=./scripts/lingbot_map_worker.py
# Torch backend: auto (default) | cpu | cuda | mps. auto prefers CUDA/ROCm, then
# Apple MPS, then CPU. Set cpu to keep a reconstruction off a GPU that is busy
# training a splat. "cuda" is also what a ROCm torch wants — there is no separate
# AMD value. The manifest's params.resolvedDevice records what actually ran,
# which is not always what was asked for: an unreachable backend is downgraded
# to CPU rather than failing.
#LINGBOT_MAP_DEVICE=auto

# Bearer tokens for android-remote-control-mcp (docs/DEVICE_TESTING.md — Remote-
# controlled testing section), read from each device's Settings → Access. Every
# device runs its own server with its own token, so there is one variable per
# device — a single shared ANDROID_MCP_TOKEN cannot work across the fleet.
# Only needed when Claude Code drives on-device testing over that connector.
# Set these as persistent user/shell environment variables (not a one-off
# `export`) before launching Claude Code, so .mcp.json's ${...} placeholders
# resolve and survive session restarts — see DEVICE_TESTING.md. A
# .mcp.local.json file is gitignored but Claude Code does not automatically
# merge it with .mcp.json, so it is not a working override mechanism today.
# Never commit the real values, and never hardcode them into the tracked
# .mcp.json.
#ANDROID_MCP_TOKEN_TAB_S8_ULTRA=
#ANDROID_MCP_TOKEN_TAB_S9_ULTRA=
#ANDROID_MCP_TOKEN_BEAM_PRO_1=
#ANDROID_MCP_TOKEN_BEAM_PRO=
# Optional SenseNova-Vision worker running in WSL. Install with
# scripts/setup-sensenova-wsl.sh, then restart the Deno coordinator. Paths
# below are examples — point them at wherever setup-sensenova-wsl.sh put the
# checkout and model on your machine.
#SENSENOVA_DIR=/path/to/SenseNova-Vision
#SENSENOVA_MODEL_PATH=/path/to/models/SenseNova-Vision-7B-MoT
#SENSENOVA_BASH=C:\Windows\System32\wsl.exe

# ── Panoptic scan (optional GPU) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-keyframe panoptic segmentation: every pixel labelled, so a scan yields
# both discrete objects and room surfaces. Unset -> the job reports 'skipped'
# with a remediation, exactly like BRUSH_BIN and SPLAT_DETECT_*.
# Setup + model/licence notes: docs/pipelines/panoptic-scan.md
#PANOPTIC_PYTHON=~/.venvs/panoptic/bin/python
#PANOPTIC_DIR=~/panoptic
#PANOPTIC_MODEL=facebook/mask2former-swin-large-ade-panoptic
#PANOPTIC_MAX_FRAMES=64
# Torch backend: auto (default) | cpu | cuda | mps, plus cuda:N to pin one card
# on a multi-GPU host. Same resolver as LINGBOT_MAP_DEVICE above. Worth setting
# to cpu only to keep this off a GPU that is busy — it is ~0.7 s/frame on a 4080
# and minutes per frame on CPU, and the manifest's resolvedDevice/deviceVendor
# say which one a given run got.
#PANOPTIC_DEVICE=auto
# JSON list of ADE20K labels treated as "things" (countable objects) rather than
# "stuff" (surfaces). Defaults to ade20k-things.json shipped next to the runner.
# A path that does not exist falls back to everything-is-stuff and reports
# "0 things" — the manifest's thingLabelsUsed says which list actually applied.
#PANOPTIC_THING_LABELS=./server/panoptic/ade20k-things.json

# ── Companion Account sign-in (OIDC, server/portal_auth.ts) ─────────────────
# The whole flow is inert unless this is 1/true — every route below is only read
# when it is on, and a stock install stays anonymous.
#PORTAL_OIDC_ENABLED=1
# CI-Portal identity provider base URL. Set but empty -> sign-in stays disabled
# with a warning at boot.
#OIDC_ISSUER_URL=https://portal.example.com
# Must exactly match the client registration in CI-Portal. Renaming it breaks
# "Sign in with Companion Account" until the IdP registration changes too.
#OIDC_CLIENT_ID=ci-web-xr-scan
# JWKS endpoint for verifying the Portal JWT. Defaults to $OIDC_ISSUER_URL/api/auth/jwks.
#PORTAL_OIDC_JWKS_URI=https://portal.example.com/api/auth/jwks
# Comma-separated accepted `aud` values. Defaults to $OIDC_CLIENT_ID.
#PORTAL_OIDC_AUDIENCE=ci-web-xr-scan
# Comma-separated allowlist of signed-in emails. FAIL-CLOSED: an empty list
# authorizes nobody, so this must be set for anyone to get in once sign-in is on.
#PORTAL_OIDC_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com,someone@example.com
# Externally reachable origin used to build the OIDC redirect_uri. Unset, it is
# derived from x-forwarded-proto/host (or the request URL) — set it when this
# server sits behind a proxy that does not forward those.
#APP_BASE_URL=https://your-machine.your-tailnet.ts.net:8000
# Signs the session cookie. Unset, a random secret is generated per boot, so
# every restart logs everyone out — set it for any deployment you restart.
#SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Companion Memory push (memory-push job, server/memory_push.ts) ──────────
# The push is inert until BOTH the URL and the token are present — a stock
# install and every existing deployment are unaffected. Normally injected by the
# Hub once the user connects the app, rather than set by hand.
#CI_SERVER_URL=https://ci-server.example.com
#CI_SERVER_TOKEN=
# Namespaces the idempotency keys sent to CI-Server. Changing it makes every
# previously-pushed memory look new, so re-pushes duplicate instead of deduping.
#MEMORY_PUSH_SOURCE_SYSTEM=companion-scan
# Per-request timeout in ms (default 120000).
#MEMORY_PUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# Per-attachment upload cap in bytes (default 536870912 = 512 MiB). CI-Server
# itself accepts up to 10 GiB; the low default keeps a runaway file from
# stalling the job for minutes.
#MEMORY_PUSH_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=536870912

# ── Splat training: backend selection (splat-train job) ────────────────────
# Which trainer the splat-train job spawns: brush (default) | spirula. It stays
# ONE job type with one manifest shape and one <base>.splat.ply, so splat-finish
# and splat-detect are unaffected by the choice — only the child process differs
# (server/main.ts:230-232, ADR 0007). Anything unrecognised falls back to brush
# rather than failing the job, so a typo cannot stop a fleet reconstructing.
#SPLAT_BACKEND=brush

# ── Brush backend (the default) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Brush binary. Default ~/brush/target/release/brush-cli; unset/absent -> the job
# reports 'skipped' with a remediation (docs/pipelines/splat-train.md — Setup).
#BRUSH_BIN=~/brush/target/release/brush-cli
# wgpu device pin. Default empty, meaning no pin — wgpu picks the default adapter.
#BRUSH_DEVICE=
#BRUSH_TRAIN_ITERS=15000
#BRUSH_SH_DEGREE=2
# Max training image resolution (default 1280).
#BRUSH_MAX_RES=1280
# splat-train solves camera poses first when the session has none. Set to 0 to
# disable that and require a colmap-poses job to have run. Read by BOTH backends
# (splat_train.ts:203, spirula_train.ts:244) — it is not a Brush-only knob.
#SPLAT_TRAIN_AUTO_POSES=0

# ── Spirula Studio backend (SPLAT_BACKEND=spirula) ─────────────────────────
# Spirula Studio is GPL-3.0 and is NOT vendored, built, or bundled here: it is a
# separately-downloaded prebuilt binary invoked as a child process over argv and
# files, exactly as Brush and COLMAP are. Read ADR 0007 before changing that
# arrangement — docs/adr/0007-splat-backends-are-selectable-and-a-rotation-only-
# sweep-is-not-a-reconstruction-capture.md.
# Spirula binary. Unset, it tries ~/opt/spirula/spirula, ~/.local/bin/spirula,
# /usr/local/bin/spirula in that order (FALLBACK_SPIRULA_BINS); none runnable ->
# the job reports 'skipped' with a download remediation, never a crash.
#SPIRULA_BIN=~/opt/spirula/spirula
# `--device`: a GPU index or a name substring (Spirula prints its device list at
# startup). Empty (default) means no pin — it picks its own adapter. Same
# reasoning as BRUSH_DEVICE: a hardcoded index only matches one machine.
#SPIRULA_DEVICE=
# Training iterations and SH degree. Defaults 15000 / 2 — deliberately Brush's
# numbers, so a Brush-vs-Spirula A/B is like-for-like. Spirula's own SH default
# is 3.
#SPIRULA_TRAIN_ITERS=15000
#SPIRULA_SH_DEGREE=2
# Training preset (the first positional arg to `spirula train`; default 3dgs)
# and `--quality`, the splat budget: low | medium (default) | high | ultra.
#SPIRULA_PRESET=3dgs
#SPIRULA_QUALITY=medium

# ── COLMAP pose solve (colmap-poses job) ───────────────────────────────────
# CPU counterpart to lingbot-map-reconstruct: writes the poses.json + seed cloud
# that splat-train's dataset builder consumes. Only the bare default 'colmap'
# triggers a search — PATH first, then ~/.local/micromamba/envs/colmap/bin/colmap
# (FALLBACK_COLMAP_BINS). An explicit COLMAP_BIN is taken at its word and only
# probed. Nothing runnable -> 'skipped', never a crash.
#COLMAP_BIN=colmap
# FeatureMatcherType is an enum COLMAP parses strictly: 'SIFT' is the only value
# the conda-forge 3.13.0 build accepts, and SIFT_BRUTEFORCE / BRUTE_FORCE /
# ALIKED / LIGHTGLUE all exit 1 on an unknown-string-value parse error
# (probed — see colmap_poses.ts:190-199). SIFT matching is brute force by
# default anyway, so 'SIFT' asks for the behaviour the longer name meant.
#COLMAP_FEATURE_TYPE=SIFT
#COLMAP_MATCHER_TYPE=SIFT
#COLMAP_MAX_IMAGE_SIZE=1600
# Quality gates on the finished solve. A solve that ran, exited 0, and wrote a
# real poses.json is still 'ok'; these thresholds attach the measurement that
# says whether the poses are usable, as quality.{registrationRatio,degenerate,
# status,reasons} in colmap-poses.manifest.json.
# Fraction of offered keyframes that must end up posed (default 0.6). Below it,
# more than a third of the walked path has no pose at all. Chosen on what it
# means, not on where two runs split — re-derive it once there are enough runs
# to plot ratio against whether the splat was usable (colmap_poses.ts:204-236).
#COLMAP_MIN_REGISTRATION_RATIO=0.6
# Triangulated-point floor (default 100); 0 disables the check. Deliberately the
# weaker of the two gates — it only ever catches a model with no structure at
# all, and the ratio does the real work.
#COLMAP_MIN_POINTS3D=100

# ── Splat detection (OWLv2 via splat_analyzer; splat-detect job) ───────────
#SPLAT_DETECT_PYTHON=~/.venvs/splat-analyzer/bin/python
#SPLAT_DETECT_DIR=~/splat_analyzer
# Open-vocabulary prompt. Unset uses the house default in server/splat/splat_detect.ts.
#SPLAT_DETECT_PROMPT=
# Camera coverage preset: low (24 views) | medium (90) | high (192).
#SPLAT_DETECT_QUALITY=medium
# Clustering knobs. Leave unset to use run_local.py's own defaults (0.12 / 8 /
# 0.40) — override only once calibrated against a real room splat.
#SPLAT_DETECT_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.12
#SPLAT_DETECT_MIN_VOTES=8
#SPLAT_DETECT_MIN_PEAK_SCORE=0.40
# GPU pin passed to the analyzer's torch/HIP process (default "1").
#HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=1
# Point-renderer splat size used while rasterizing detection views (default "6").
#POINT_SPLAT_SIZE=6

# ── Splat finishing (@playcanvas/splat-transform; splat-finish job) ────────
# A splat-finish job is auto-enqueued when splat-train completes ok. Set to 0 as
# the kill switch.
#SPLAT_FINISH_AUTO=0
# node binary used to run the CLI (default "node").
#SPLAT_FINISH_NODE=node
# splat-transform cli.mjs. Defaults to the copy in this repo's node_modules;
# absent there too -> the job reports 'skipped' with an install remediation.
#SPLAT_TRANSFORM_CLI=./node_modules/@playcanvas/splat-transform/bin/cli.mjs
# SOG compression device: "cpu" (default) or an adapter index.
#SPLAT_FINISH_GPU=cpu
# SH-compression k-means iterations (default 10).
#SPLAT_FINISH_SH_ITERS=10

# ── Dev + CI scripts (scripts/*.mjs) ───────────────────────────────────────
# None of these are read by the Deno server. They are listed because they were
# undocumented and each one is a knob someone has already had to discover by
# reading the script.
# Base URL the corpus/eval/audit scripts POST to (eval-cv.mjs, seed-test-homes.mjs,
# audit-scan-glb.mjs). Default http://localhost:8000.
#SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8000
# `npm run eval:cv` — which ground-truth corpus to score against ('real' by
# default, 'synthetic' for the mock-engine regression fixture), and whether a
# regression is a hard failure (STRICT=1 exits non-zero; ci-mock-gate.mjs sets it).
#CV_CORPUS=real
#STRICT=1
# `npm run bench:models` — comma-separated Ollama tags to score (default: the
# first model per catalog tier), the throwaway API port it boots, whether a tag
# missing from the local Ollama store is pulled rather than skipped, and how long
# to let a cold model warm up before timing it.
#BENCH_MODELS=
#BENCH_PORT=8095
#BENCH_PULL=1
#BENCH_WARMUP_MS=300000
# `npm run setup:models` — non-interactive selection (comma-separated catalog
# tags, or 'all'); set to any value, including empty, and the prompt is skipped.
# SETUP_MODELS_DRY_RUN=1 prints the plan and pulls nothing.
#SETUP_MODELS=qwen3-vl:8b
#SETUP_MODELS_DRY_RUN=1
# `npm run seed:test-data` — how many generated homes to seed (default: all) and
# an explicit comma-separated subset by name.
#COUNT=3
#HOMES=
# `npm run links:house` — which world manifest to read the share tokens from.
# Default worlds/default.json.
#WORLD_FILE=./worlds/default.json
# `npm run test:lingbot:fetch-video` — override the Big Buck Bunny source URL the
# LingBot fixture is cut from (CC-BY 3.0; provenance is written next to it).
#SOURCE_URL=
# `npm run test:box` — isolated ports for the self-contained browser loop, so it
# never collides with the long-lived :8000/:5173 servers.
#TEST_BOX_API_PORT=8100
#TEST_BOX_VITE_PORT=5273
# scripts/video-stage.mjs — the product-video capture stage. Its own isolated
# knobs, deliberately NOT the app-wide PORT/VITE_PORT: several agents stage
# different repos at once. VIDEO_SCANS is how many generated homes get placed
# adjacent for the shot; VIDEO_DATA_DIR keeps scans/worlds churn out of the
# checkout (default a dir under the system temp dir).
#VIDEO_PORT=5173
#VIDEO_API_PORT=8000
#VIDEO_SCANS=3
#VIDEO_DATA_DIR=/tmp/ci-video-scan-stage
# scripts/tablet-user-suite.mjs — the on-device MCP suite. Unset, it reads the
# url + Authorization header out of .mcp.local.json for the server named by
# TABLET_MCP_SERVER (default android-tab-s9-ultra). TABLET_MCP_BEARER is the bare
# token; TABLET_MCP_AUTH is a full header value. TABLET_TEST_URL overrides the
# app URL under test (falling back to VITE_URL, then the resolved tailnet host).
# TABLET_KEEP_GOING=1 is the same as passing --keep-going.
#TABLET_MCP_CONFIG=.mcp.local.json
#TABLET_MCP_SERVER=android-tab-s9-ultra
#TABLET_MCP_URL=
#TABLET_MCP_BEARER=
#TABLET_MCP_AUTH=
#TABLET_TEST_URL=
#TABLET_KEEP_GOING=1
# scripts/check-detection-gate.mjs — the ref the detection baseline is diffed
# against. BASE_REF wins; GITHUB_BASE_REF (set by GitHub Actions) is used as
# `origin/$GITHUB_BASE_REF`; otherwise origin/main.
#BASE_REF=origin/main
